(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("proxies",{"proxies/what-a-reverse-proxy-does":{"html":"\u003cp>Almost every production web service sits behind a proxy. It is the front door: it decides which backend gets each request and enforces rules before the traffic reaches your code. Understanding its jobs makes every later topic, from TLS to timeouts, easier.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The difference between forward proxies, reverse proxies and load balancers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The jobs a reverse proxy does\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Layer 4 and layer 7 proxying\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Where the proxy sits in an architecture\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"forward-reverse-and-load-balancer\">Forward, reverse and load balancer\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>forward proxy\u003c/strong> sits on the client side and sends requests out for users (for example a company web filter). A \u003cstrong>reverse proxy\u003c/strong> sits on the server side and receives requests for your services. A \u003cstrong>load balancer\u003c/strong> is a reverse proxy whose main job is to spread requests across several backends. In practice one program, such as nginx, HAProxy or Envoy, often does all three roles.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"jobs-of-a-reverse-proxy\">Jobs of a reverse proxy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Routing:\u003c/strong> send \u003ccode>api.example.com\u003c/code> to one service and \u003ccode>/static/\u003c/code> to another.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>TLS termination:\u003c/strong> handle certificates and encryption once, speak plain or re-encrypted traffic inside.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Load balancing and health checks:\u003c/strong> share requests and stop sending to failed backends.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Protection:\u003c/strong> rate limits, size limits, request filtering and hiding backend details.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Performance:\u003c/strong> compression, connection reuse and caching of responses.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Observability:\u003c/strong> one place to log every request.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"layer-4-and-layer-7\">Layer 4 and layer 7\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>layer 4\u003c/strong> proxy forwards TCP or UDP streams without reading them, which is fast and works for any protocol. A \u003cstrong>layer 7\u003c/strong> proxy understands HTTP, so it can route by host, path, header or cookie and can rewrite requests. Choose layer 4 for databases and raw TLS passthrough, layer 7 for web traffic.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"user&gt;proxy;proxy&gt;web;proxy&gt;api;proxy&gt;log\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A reverse proxy in front of services\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"user\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Users\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"proxy\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Reverse proxy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"web\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Web service\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"api\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">API service\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"log\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Access logs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Users to Reverse proxy; 2, Reverse proxy to Web service; 3, Reverse proxy to API service; 4, Reverse proxy to Access logs.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"user\">\u003cdt>Users \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Browsers and apps that connect to one public address.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If the address changes without DNS updates, users cannot reach you.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"proxy\">\u003cdt>Reverse proxy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Terminates TLS, applies rules and picks a backend.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A single proxy is a single point of failure; run at least two.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"web\">\u003cdt>Web service \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Serves pages for the main host.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If it fails, the proxy should stop routing to it.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"api\">\u003cdt>API service \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Serves API paths on private addresses.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Exposed directly, it skips the proxy's protections.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"log\">\u003cdt>Access logs \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Every request is recorded at the front door.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Without logs you cannot tell users from attackers.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-a-proxy-changes\">What a proxy changes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Backends now see the proxy&#39;s address, not the user&#39;s. The proxy passes the original address in headers such as \u003ccode>X-Forwarded-For\u003c/code>, and the application must trust that header only from the proxy. The proxy is also now a place where timeouts, buffer sizes and limits can break otherwise healthy requests, which later lessons cover.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-three-ports-three-certificates\">Incident: Three ports, three certificates\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A team exposed each app on its own public port and one certificate expired unnoticed.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"curl -I https://shop.example.com:8443\nss -ltn | grep -c LISTEN\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -I https://shop.example.com:8443\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">curl: (60) SSL certificate problem: certificate has expired\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ss -ltn | grep -c LISTEN\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">11\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>They moved to one proxy on port 443 with automatic certificate renewal, closed the app ports and gained one access log. Expiry alerts moved from three places to one.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Exposing backends directly as well as through the proxy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Trusting X-Forwarded-For from the whole internet\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running a single proxy with no standby\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using layer 7 rules for a protocol the proxy does not understand\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"forward-reverse-and-load-balancer","text":"Forward, reverse and load balancer"},{"id":"jobs-of-a-reverse-proxy","text":"Jobs of a reverse proxy"},{"id":"layer-4-and-layer-7","text":"Layer 4 and layer 7"},{"id":"what-a-proxy-changes","text":"What a proxy changes"},{"id":"incident-three-ports-three-certificates","text":"Incident: Three ports, three certificates"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"nginx documentation","u":"https://nginx.org/en/docs/"},{"t":"HAProxy documentation","u":"https://docs.haproxy.org/"},{"t":"Envoy documentation","u":"https://www.envoyproxy.io/docs/envoy/latest/"}],"text":"Almost every production web service sits behind a proxy. It is the front door: it decides which backend gets each request and enforces rules before the traffic reaches your code. Understanding its jobs makes every later topic, from TLS to timeouts, easier. What you will learn The difference between forward proxies, reverse proxies and load balancers The jobs a reverse proxy does Layer 4 and layer 7 proxying Where the proxy sits in an architecture Forward, reverse and load balancer A forward proxy sits on the client side and sends requests out for users (for example a company web filter). A reverse proxy sits on the server side and receives requests for your services. A load balancer is a reverse proxy whose main job is to spread requests across several backends. In practice one program, such as nginx, HAProxy or Envoy, often does all three roles. Jobs of a reverse proxy Routing: send api.example.com to one service and /static/ to another. TLS termination: handle certificates and encryption once, speak plain or re-encrypted traffic inside. Load balancing and health checks: share requests and stop sending to failed backends. Protection: rate limits, size limits, request filtering and hiding backend details. Performance: compression, connection reuse and caching of responses. Observability: one place to log every request. Layer 4 and layer 7 A layer 4 proxy forwards TCP or UDP streams without reading them, which is fast and works for any protocol. A layer 7 proxy understands HTTP, so it can route by host, path, header or cookie and can rewrite requests. Choose layer 4 for databases and raw TLS passthrough, layer 7 for web traffic. A reverse proxy in front of services Flow in order: 1, Users to Reverse proxy; 2, Reverse proxy to Web service; 3, Reverse proxy to API service; 4, Reverse proxy to Access logs. Client Network edge Service Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Users Client What it does: Browsers and apps that connect to one public address. If it fails or is weak: If the address changes without DNS updates, users cannot reach you. Reverse proxy Network edge What it does: Terminates TLS, applies rules and picks a backend. If it fails or is weak: A single proxy is a single point of failure; run at least two. Web service Service What it does: Serves pages for the main host. If it fails or is weak: If it fails, the proxy should stop routing to it. API service Service What it does: Serves API paths on private addresses. If it fails or is weak: Exposed directly, it skips the proxy's protections. Access logs Observability What it does: Every request is recorded at the front door. If it fails or is weak: Without logs you cannot tell users from attackers. What a proxy changes Backends now see the proxy&#39;s address, not the user&#39;s. The proxy passes the original address in headers such as X-Forwarded-For , and the application must trust that header only from the proxy. The proxy is also now a place where timeouts, buffer sizes and limits can break otherwise healthy requests, which later lessons cover. Incident: Three ports, three certificates Incident A team exposed each app on its own public port and one certificate expired unnoticed. $ curl -I https://shop.example.com:8443 curl: (60) SSL certificate problem: certificate has expired $ ss -ltn | grep -c LISTEN 11 They moved to one proxy on port 443 with automatic certificate renewal, closed the app ports and gained one access log. Expiry alerts moved from three places to one. Common mistakes Exposing backends directly as well as through the proxy Trusting X-Forwarded-For from the whole internet Running a single proxy with no standby Using layer 7 rules for a protocol the proxy does not understand"},"proxies/nginx-configuration-servers-and-locations":{"html":"\u003cp>nginx configuration looks simple and hides a few rules that cause most mistakes. Once you know how a server is chosen and how locations are matched, you can read any nginx file and predict what it does.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Contexts and directives in nginx.conf\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How server and server_name are chosen\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Location matching order\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Testing and reloading safely\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"contexts\">Contexts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"nginx\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"events { worker_connections 1024; }\nhttp {\n  include mime.types;\n  server {\n    listen 80;\n    server_name shop.example.com;\n    root /var/www/shop;\n\n    location / { try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html; }\n    location /api/ { proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080; }\n    location = /healthz { return 200 &quot;ok\\n&quot;; }\n    location ~* \\.(png|jpg|css|js)$ { expires 7d; }\n  }\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">events\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">worker_connections\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1024\u003c/span>; }\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">http\u003c/span> {\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">include\u003c/span> mime.types;\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">server\u003c/span> {\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">listen\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">80\u003c/span>;\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">server_name\u003c/span> shop.example.com;\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">root\u003c/span> /var/www/shop;\n\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">location\u003c/span> / { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">try_files\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$uri\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$uri\u003c/span>/ /index.html; }\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">location\u003c/span> /api/ { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">proxy_pass\u003c/span> http://127.0.0.1:8080; }\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">location\u003c/span> = /healthz { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">return\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">200\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ok\\n&quot;\u003c/span>; }\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">location\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-regexp\">~* \\.(png|jpg|css|js)$\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">expires\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">7d\u003c/span>; }\n  }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>events\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>http\u003c/code> are top-level contexts. \u003ccode>server\u003c/code> lives in \u003ccode>http\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>location\u003c/code> in \u003ccode>server\u003c/code>. A directive set in an outer context is inherited unless an inner block sets it again.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"choosing-the-server\">Choosing the server\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>nginx matches the \u003ccode>listen\u003c/code> address and port first, then compares the request&#39;s \u003ccode>Host\u003c/code> header with \u003ccode>server_name\u003c/code>. If nothing matches it uses the default server for that port (the first one, or the one marked \u003ccode>default_server\u003c/code>). A catch-all default that returns 444 or 404 stops unknown hostnames from reaching a real site.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"location-matching\">Location matching\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Form\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Priority\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>location = /x\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Exact match\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Highest, stops searching\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>location ^~ /x\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Prefix, skip regexes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Stops regex checks\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>location ~ re\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>~* re\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Regex (case sensitive or not)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Checked in file order\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>location /x\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Prefix\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Longest prefix remembered, lowest\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>nginx finds the longest matching prefix, then checks regexes in order, and a matching regex wins over a plain prefix. That is why a broad image regex can capture \u003ccode>/api/photo.png\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"req&gt;srv;srv&gt;loc;loc&gt;act;test&gt;srv\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How nginx picks where a request goes\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"req\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Request\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"srv\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Server block\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"loc\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Location\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"act\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Action\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"test\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">nginx -t\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Request to Server block; 2, Server block to Location; 3, Location to Action; 4, nginx -t to Server block.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"req\">\u003cdt>Request \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Arrives with an address, port, Host header and path.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A missing Host header falls back to the default server.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"srv\">\u003cdt>Server block \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Chosen by listen and server_name.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No default_server means the first block answers unknown hosts.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"loc\">\u003cdt>Location \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Exact, then prefix, then regex rules choose a location.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A broad regex can steal paths from a prefix.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"act\">\u003cdt>Action \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Serve a file, proxy_pass, return or redirect.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A wrong proxy_pass trailing slash rewrites the path.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"test\">\u003cdt>nginx -t \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Checks the syntax before reload.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Reloading without a test can take the site down.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"test-then-reload\">Test, then reload\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>nginx -t\u003c/code> reports syntax and file errors. \u003ccode>nginx -s reload\u003c/code> starts new workers with the new configuration and lets old ones finish their requests, so no connection is dropped. If the test fails, the old configuration keeps running.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-regex-that-stole-the-api\">Incident: The regex that stole the API\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new image location used a regex that also matched paths under /api/.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"nginx -t\ncurl -sI https://shop.example.com/api/photo.png | head -1\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>nginx -t\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">nginx: configuration file /etc/nginx/nginx.conf test is successful\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -sI https://shop.example.com/api/photo.png | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">HTTP/1.1 404 Not Found\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The syntax was valid, so the test passed. The request matched the regex location before the /api/ prefix, and was served from disk. Adding ^~ to the API location stopped regex matching for that prefix.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Assuming the first location in the file wins\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reloading without running nginx -t\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Having no default server for unknown hosts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Repeating the same directive in every block instead of using inheritance\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"contexts","text":"Contexts"},{"id":"choosing-the-server","text":"Choosing the server"},{"id":"location-matching","text":"Location matching"},{"id":"test-then-reload","text":"Test, then reload"},{"id":"incident-the-regex-that-stole-the-api","text":"Incident: The regex that stole the API"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"nginx beginner's guide","u":"https://nginx.org/en/docs/beginners_guide.html"},{"t":"How nginx processes a request","u":"https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/request_processing.html"}],"text":"nginx configuration looks simple and hides a few rules that cause most mistakes. Once you know how a server is chosen and how locations are matched, you can read any nginx file and predict what it does. What you will learn Contexts and directives in nginx.conf How server and server_name are chosen Location matching order Testing and reloading safely Contexts events { worker_connections 1024 ; } http { include mime.types; server { listen 80 ; server_name shop.example.com; root /var/www/shop; location / { try_files $uri $uri / /index.html; } location /api/ { proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080; } location = /healthz { return 200 \"ok\\n\" ; } location ~* \\.(png|jpg|css|js)$ { expires 7d ; } } } events and http are top-level contexts. server lives in http , location in server . A directive set in an outer context is inherited unless an inner block sets it again. Choosing the server nginx matches the listen address and port first, then compares the request&#39;s Host header with server_name . If nothing matches it uses the default server for that port (the first one, or the one marked default_server ). A catch-all default that returns 444 or 404 stops unknown hostnames from reaching a real site. Location matching Form Meaning Priority location = /x Exact match Highest, stops searching location ^~ /x Prefix, skip regexes Stops regex checks location ~ re / ~* re Regex (case sensitive or not) Checked in file order location /x Prefix Longest prefix remembered, lowest nginx finds the longest matching prefix, then checks regexes in order, and a matching regex wins over a plain prefix. That is why a broad image regex can capture /api/photo.png . How nginx picks where a request goes Flow in order: 1, Request to Server block; 2, Server block to Location; 3, Location to Action; 4, nginx -t to Server block. Step Service Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Request Step What it does: Arrives with an address, port, Host header and path. If it fails or is weak: A missing Host header falls back to the default server. Server block Service What it does: Chosen by listen and server_name. If it fails or is weak: No default_server means the first block answers unknown hosts. Location Step What it does: Exact, then prefix, then regex rules choose a location. If it fails or is weak: A broad regex can steal paths from a prefix. Action Step What it does: Serve a file, proxy_pass, return or redirect. If it fails or is weak: A wrong proxy_pass trailing slash rewrites the path. nginx -t Observability What it does: Checks the syntax before reload. If it fails or is weak: Reloading without a test can take the site down. Test, then reload nginx -t reports syntax and file errors. nginx -s reload starts new workers with the new configuration and lets old ones finish their requests, so no connection is dropped. If the test fails, the old configuration keeps running. Incident: The regex that stole the API Incident A new image location used a regex that also matched paths under /api/. $ nginx -t nginx: configuration file /etc/nginx/nginx.conf test is successful $ curl -sI https://shop.example.com/api/photo.png | head -1 HTTP/1.1 404 Not Found The syntax was valid, so the test passed. The request matched the regex location before the /api/ prefix, and was served from disk. Adding ^~ to the API location stopped regex matching for that prefix. Common mistakes Assuming the first location in the file wins Reloading without running nginx -t Having no default server for unknown hosts Repeating the same directive in every block instead of using inheritance"},"proxies/proxying-to-upstreams-headers-and-buffers":{"html":"\u003cp>A proxy that simply passes bytes along is not enough. Applications use the Host header, the client address and the scheme to build links, set cookies and log users. Getting these headers right, and understanding buffering, prevents a long list of confusing bugs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>proxy_pass and how paths are rewritten\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Headers the backend needs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Buffering, streaming and large uploads\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Proxying WebSockets and server-sent events\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"proxy-pass-and-the-trailing-slash\">proxy_pass and the trailing slash\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"nginx\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"location /api/ {\n    proxy_pass http://app_backend;          # sends /api/users as /api/users\n}\nlocation /v1/ {\n    proxy_pass http://app_backend/;         # sends /v1/users as /users (prefix replaced)\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">location\u003c/span> /api/ {\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">proxy_pass\u003c/span> http://app_backend;          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># sends /api/users as /api/users\u003c/span>\n}\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">location\u003c/span> /v1/ {\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">proxy_pass\u003c/span> http://app_backend/;         \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># sends /v1/users as /users (prefix replaced)\u003c/span>\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>With no URI after the host, the original path is sent. With a URI (even just \u003ccode>/\u003c/code>), the matched prefix is replaced. One character changes what the backend receives.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"headers\">Headers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"nginx\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"proxy_set_header Host              $host;\nproxy_set_header X-Real-IP         $remote_addr;\nproxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For   $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;\nproxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">proxy_set_header\u003c/span> Host              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$host\u003c/span>;\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">proxy_set_header\u003c/span> X-Real-IP         \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$remote_addr\u003c/span>;\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">proxy_set_header\u003c/span> X-Forwarded-For   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$proxy_add_x_forwarded_for\u003c/span>;\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">proxy_set_header\u003c/span> X-Forwarded-Proto \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$scheme\u003c/span>;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The app should trust these headers only when the request comes from the proxy&#39;s address. Otherwise anyone can claim to be any client by sending the header.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"buffering\">Buffering\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>By default nginx reads the whole response from the backend into buffers and then sends it at the client&#39;s pace, which frees the backend quickly. For streaming responses and server-sent events turn it off with \u003ccode>proxy_buffering off;\u003c/code>. Large uploads are limited by \u003ccode>client_max_body_size\u003c/code> (default 1 MB), which returns 413 when exceeded.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"websockets\">WebSockets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"nginx\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"map $http_upgrade $connection_upgrade { default upgrade; '' close; }\nlocation /ws/ {\n    proxy_pass http://app_backend;\n    proxy_http_version 1.1;\n    proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;\n    proxy_set_header Connection $connection_upgrade;\n    proxy_read_timeout 3600s;\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">map\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$http_upgrade\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$connection_upgrade\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">default\u003c/span> upgrade; &#x27;&#x27; close; }\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">location\u003c/span> /ws/ {\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">proxy_pass\u003c/span> http://app_backend;\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">proxy_http_version\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>.\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>;\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">proxy_set_header\u003c/span> Upgrade \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$http_upgrade\u003c/span>;\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">proxy_set_header\u003c/span> Connection \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$connection_upgrade\u003c/span>;\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">proxy_read_timeout\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3600s\u003c/span>;\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"client&gt;proxy;proxy&gt;app;proxy&gt;ws\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">What the proxy tells the backend\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"client\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Client\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"proxy\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">nginx\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Backend app\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ws\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">WebSocket\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Client to nginx; 2, nginx to Backend app; 3, nginx to WebSocket.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"client\">\u003cdt>Client \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Sends Host, cookies and its own address.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> The client can also forge headers such as X-Forwarded-For.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"proxy\">\u003cdt>nginx \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Rewrites the request and sets trusted forwarding headers.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If it forgets Host, the app builds wrong links.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>Backend app \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Reads Host, scheme and client address from headers.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Trusting headers from anyone but the proxy is a spoofing hole.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ws\">\u003cdt>WebSocket \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A long-lived upgraded connection through the same proxy.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Default timeouts close idle sockets after 60 seconds.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-redirects-to-localhost\">Incident: Redirects to localhost\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After moving behind nginx a login redirected users to \u003ca href=\"http://localhost:3000/dashboard\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">http://localhost:3000/dashboard\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"curl -sI https://shop.example.com/login\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -sI https://shop.example.com/login\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">HTTP/1.1 302 Found\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Location: http://localhost:3000/dashboard\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The app built the absolute URL from the Host and scheme it received, which were the proxy&#39;s connection to localhost. Passing Host and X-Forwarded-Proto and telling the app to trust the proxy fixed the redirect.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Changing proxy_pass without noticing the trailing-slash rule\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving the default 1 MB upload limit on an upload endpoint\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting WebSocket upgrade headers and timeouts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Letting any client send X-Forwarded-For that the app trusts\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"proxy-pass-and-the-trailing-slash","text":"proxy_pass and the trailing slash"},{"id":"headers","text":"Headers"},{"id":"buffering","text":"Buffering"},{"id":"websockets","text":"WebSockets"},{"id":"incident-redirects-to-localhost","text":"Incident: Redirects to localhost"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"ngx_http_proxy_module","u":"https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_proxy_module.html"},{"t":"WebSocket proxying","u":"https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/websocket.html"}],"text":"A proxy that simply passes bytes along is not enough. Applications use the Host header, the client address and the scheme to build links, set cookies and log users. Getting these headers right, and understanding buffering, prevents a long list of confusing bugs. What you will learn proxy_pass and how paths are rewritten Headers the backend needs Buffering, streaming and large uploads Proxying WebSockets and server-sent events proxy_pass and the trailing slash location /api/ { proxy_pass http://app_backend; # sends /api/users as /api/users } location /v1/ { proxy_pass http://app_backend/; # sends /v1/users as /users (prefix replaced) } With no URI after the host, the original path is sent. With a URI (even just / ), the matched prefix is replaced. One character changes what the backend receives. Headers proxy_set_header Host $host ; proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr ; proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for ; proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme ; The app should trust these headers only when the request comes from the proxy&#39;s address. Otherwise anyone can claim to be any client by sending the header. Buffering By default nginx reads the whole response from the backend into buffers and then sends it at the client&#39;s pace, which frees the backend quickly. For streaming responses and server-sent events turn it off with proxy_buffering off; . Large uploads are limited by client_max_body_size (default 1 MB), which returns 413 when exceeded. WebSockets map $http_upgrade $connection_upgrade { default upgrade; &#x27;&#x27; close; } location /ws/ { proxy_pass http://app_backend; proxy_http_version 1 . 1 ; proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade ; proxy_set_header Connection $connection_upgrade ; proxy_read_timeout 3600s ; } What the proxy tells the backend Flow in order: 1, Client to nginx; 2, nginx to Backend app; 3, nginx to WebSocket. Client Step Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Client Client What it does: Sends Host, cookies and its own address. If it fails or is weak: The client can also forge headers such as X-Forwarded-For. nginx Step What it does: Rewrites the request and sets trusted forwarding headers. If it fails or is weak: If it forgets Host, the app builds wrong links. Backend app Service What it does: Reads Host, scheme and client address from headers. If it fails or is weak: Trusting headers from anyone but the proxy is a spoofing hole. WebSocket Step What it does: A long-lived upgraded connection through the same proxy. If it fails or is weak: Default timeouts close idle sockets after 60 seconds. Incident: Redirects to localhost Incident After moving behind nginx a login redirected users to http://localhost:3000/dashboard . $ curl -sI https://shop.example.com/login HTTP/1.1 302 Found Location: http://localhost:3000/dashboard The app built the absolute URL from the Host and scheme it received, which were the proxy&#39;s connection to localhost. Passing Host and X-Forwarded-Proto and telling the app to trust the proxy fixed the redirect. Common mistakes Changing proxy_pass without noticing the trailing-slash rule Leaving the default 1 MB upload limit on an upload endpoint Forgetting WebSocket upgrade headers and timeouts Letting any client send X-Forwarded-For that the app trusts"},"proxies/tls-termination-http2-and-certificates":{"html":"\u003cp>The proxy is where users meet TLS. A good setup is short: a valid chain, modern versions, a redirect from plain HTTP and renewal that nobody has to remember. A bad one fails on a date you cannot see.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A minimal HTTPS server block\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Protocols, ciphers and HTTP/2\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>HSTS, redirects and OCSP stapling\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Automated renewal and re-encryption to backends\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-minimal-https-server\">A minimal HTTPS server\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"nginx\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"server { listen 80; server_name shop.example.com; return 301 https://$host$request_uri; }\nserver {\n    listen 443 ssl;\n    http2 on;\n    server_name shop.example.com;\n    ssl_certificate     /etc/ssl/shop/fullchain.pem;\n    ssl_certificate_key /etc/ssl/shop/privkey.pem;\n    ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;\n    ssl_session_cache shared:SSL:10m;\n    add_header Strict-Transport-Security &quot;max-age=31536000&quot; always;\n    location / { proxy_pass http://app_backend; }\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">server\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">listen\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">80\u003c/span>; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">server_name\u003c/span> shop.example.com; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">return\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">301\u003c/span> https://\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$host\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$request_uri\u003c/span>; }\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">server\u003c/span> {\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">listen\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">443\u003c/span> ssl;\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">http2\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">on\u003c/span>;\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">server_name\u003c/span> shop.example.com;\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">ssl_certificate\u003c/span>     /etc/ssl/shop/fullchain.pem;\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">ssl_certificate_key\u003c/span> /etc/ssl/shop/privkey.pem;\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">ssl_protocols\u003c/span> TLSv1.\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span> TLSv1.\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>;\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">ssl_session_cache\u003c/span> shared:SSL:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10m\u003c/span>;\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">add_header\u003c/span> Strict-Transport-Security \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;max-age=31536000&quot;\u003c/span> always;\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">location\u003c/span> / { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">proxy_pass\u003c/span> http://app_backend; }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use the \u003cstrong>full chain\u003c/strong> (your certificate plus intermediates). A missing intermediate works in some browsers and fails in others and in \u003ccode>curl\u003c/code>. Keep the private key readable only by root.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"modern-settings\">Modern settings\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>TLS 1.2 and 1.3 are enough; older versions are retired. HTTP/2 multiplexes many requests over one connection and is enabled with \u003ccode>http2 on\u003c/code>. \u003cstrong>HSTS\u003c/strong> tells browsers to use HTTPS only for a period, so start with a short value before committing to a year. \u003cstrong>OCSP stapling\u003c/strong> lets the server include proof that the certificate is not revoked, which speeds up connections.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"renewal\">Renewal\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Most public certificates come from an ACME service. A client such as certbot or acme.sh proves control of the domain, fetches a certificate and runs a hook to reload the proxy. Test renewal with a dry run, alert at 21 days to expiry, and monitor the certificate the proxy actually serves, not just the file on disk.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"acme&gt;cert;cert&gt;proxy;proxy&gt;users;mon&gt;proxy\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Certificate renewal that does not depend on a person\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"acme\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">ACME client\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cert\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Certificate files\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"proxy\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Proxy reload\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"users\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Users\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"mon\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Expiry monitor\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, ACME client to Certificate files; 2, Certificate files to Proxy reload; 3, Proxy reload to Users; 4, Expiry monitor to Proxy reload.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"acme\">\u003cdt>ACME client \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Requests and renews certificates automatically.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If the job fails silently, expiry arrives unnoticed.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cert\">\u003cdt>Certificate files \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> fullchain.pem and a protected private key on the proxy.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A wrong chain order breaks non-browser clients.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"proxy\">\u003cdt>Proxy reload \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A deploy hook reloads nginx so it serves the new file.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Renewed on disk but not reloaded still serves the old one.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"users\">\u003cdt>Users \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Browsers and clients see the live certificate.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An expired certificate blocks access completely.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"mon\">\u003cdt>Expiry monitor \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Checks the served certificate and alerts early.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No monitor means the first alert is a customer.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"re-encrypting-to-backends\">Re-encrypting to backends\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If the network between proxy and app is not fully trusted, use \u003ccode>proxy_pass https://...\u003c/code> and verify the backend certificate with \u003ccode>proxy_ssl_verify on\u003c/code>. Otherwise plain HTTP inside a private network is common, but the choice should be conscious and written down.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-certificate-that-renewed-but-did-not-reload\">Incident: The certificate that renewed but did not reload\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A renewal ran on schedule but the proxy kept serving the old certificate until it expired.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"echo | openssl s_client -connect shop.example.com:443 2&gt;/dev/null | openssl x509 -noout -enddate\nls -l /etc/ssl/shop/fullchain.pem\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> | openssl s_client -connect shop.example.com:443 2&gt;/dev/null | openssl x509 -noout -enddate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">notAfter=Nov  2 03:00:00 2026 GMT\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> -l /etc/ssl/shop/fullchain.pem\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-rw-r--r-- 1 root root 5412 Oct 28 03:00 fullchain.pem\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The file on disk was new but nginx had never reloaded. The team added a post-renewal hook that runs nginx -t and reload, and a monitor that checks the live certificate&#39;s expiry.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Serving the leaf certificate without the intermediates\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Checking only the file on disk, not the served certificate\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Setting a one-year HSTS before the site is fully on HTTPS\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving old TLS versions enabled for no reason\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"a-minimal-https-server","text":"A minimal HTTPS server"},{"id":"modern-settings","text":"Modern settings"},{"id":"renewal","text":"Renewal"},{"id":"re-encrypting-to-backends","text":"Re-encrypting to backends"},{"id":"incident-the-certificate-that-renewed-but-did-not-reload","text":"Incident: The certificate that renewed but did not reload"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Configuring HTTPS servers","u":"https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/configuring_https_servers.html"},{"t":"Mozilla SSL configuration generator","u":"https://ssl-config.mozilla.org/"}],"text":"The proxy is where users meet TLS. A good setup is short: a valid chain, modern versions, a redirect from plain HTTP and renewal that nobody has to remember. A bad one fails on a date you cannot see. What you will learn A minimal HTTPS server block Protocols, ciphers and HTTP/2 HSTS, redirects and OCSP stapling Automated renewal and re-encryption to backends A minimal HTTPS server server { listen 80 ; server_name shop.example.com; return 301 https:// $host $request_uri ; } server { listen 443 ssl; http2 on ; server_name shop.example.com; ssl_certificate /etc/ssl/shop/fullchain.pem; ssl_certificate_key /etc/ssl/shop/privkey.pem; ssl_protocols TLSv1. 2 TLSv1. 3 ; ssl_session_cache shared:SSL: 10m ; add_header Strict-Transport-Security \"max-age=31536000\" always; location / { proxy_pass http://app_backend; } } Use the full chain (your certificate plus intermediates). A missing intermediate works in some browsers and fails in others and in curl . Keep the private key readable only by root. Modern settings TLS 1.2 and 1.3 are enough; older versions are retired. HTTP/2 multiplexes many requests over one connection and is enabled with http2 on . HSTS tells browsers to use HTTPS only for a period, so start with a short value before committing to a year. OCSP stapling lets the server include proof that the certificate is not revoked, which speeds up connections. Renewal Most public certificates come from an ACME service. A client such as certbot or acme.sh proves control of the domain, fetches a certificate and runs a hook to reload the proxy. Test renewal with a dry run, alert at 21 days to expiry, and monitor the certificate the proxy actually serves, not just the file on disk. Certificate renewal that does not depend on a person Flow in order: 1, ACME client to Certificate files; 2, Certificate files to Proxy reload; 3, Proxy reload to Users; 4, Expiry monitor to Proxy reload. Client Security Network edge Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. ACME client Client What it does: Requests and renews certificates automatically. If it fails or is weak: If the job fails silently, expiry arrives unnoticed. Certificate files Security What it does: fullchain.pem and a protected private key on the proxy. If it fails or is weak: A wrong chain order breaks non-browser clients. Proxy reload Network edge What it does: A deploy hook reloads nginx so it serves the new file. If it fails or is weak: Renewed on disk but not reloaded still serves the old one. Users Client What it does: Browsers and clients see the live certificate. If it fails or is weak: An expired certificate blocks access completely. Expiry monitor Observability What it does: Checks the served certificate and alerts early. If it fails or is weak: No monitor means the first alert is a customer. Re-encrypting to backends If the network between proxy and app is not fully trusted, use proxy_pass https://... and verify the backend certificate with proxy_ssl_verify on . Otherwise plain HTTP inside a private network is common, but the choice should be conscious and written down. Incident: The certificate that renewed but did not reload Incident A renewal ran on schedule but the proxy kept serving the old certificate until it expired. $ echo | openssl s_client -connect shop.example.com:443 2>/dev/null | openssl x509 -noout -enddate notAfter=Nov 2 03:00:00 2026 GMT $ ls -l /etc/ssl/shop/fullchain.pem -rw-r--r-- 1 root root 5412 Oct 28 03:00 fullchain.pem The file on disk was new but nginx had never reloaded. The team added a post-renewal hook that runs nginx -t and reload, and a monitor that checks the live certificate&#39;s expiry. Common mistakes Serving the leaf certificate without the intermediates Checking only the file on disk, not the served certificate Setting a one-year HSTS before the site is fully on HTTPS Leaving old TLS versions enabled for no reason"},"proxies/load-balancing-algorithms-and-health-checks":{"html":"\u003cp>Spreading requests is easy. Doing it well means choosing a method that fits your traffic and, more importantly, knowing when a backend is sick and taking it out of rotation quickly and safely.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Round robin, weighted, least connections and hashing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Session stickiness and when to avoid it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Passive and active health checks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Draining, slow start and retries\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"algorithms\">Algorithms\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"nginx\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"upstream app_backend {\n    least_conn;\n    server 10.0.1.11:8080 weight=2 max_fails=3 fail_timeout=15s;\n    server 10.0.1.12:8080;\n    server 10.0.1.13:8080 backup;\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">upstream\u003c/span> app_backend {\n    least_conn;\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">server\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10.0.1.11:8080\u003c/span> weight=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span> max_fails=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span> fail_timeout=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">15s\u003c/span>;\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">server\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10.0.1.12:8080\u003c/span>;\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">server\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10.0.1.13:8080\u003c/span> backup;\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Method\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Good for\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Round robin\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Equal servers and short, similar requests\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Weighted\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Servers of different size\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Least connections\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Long or uneven requests\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Hash on a key (\u003ccode>hash $request_uri consistent\u003c/code>)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Caches, where the same key should reach the same server\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>ip_hash\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Simple stickiness by client address\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"stickiness\">Stickiness\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Sending a user to the same backend helps when sessions live in server memory, but it spreads load unevenly and a failed server loses its users&#39; sessions. Prefer to keep session data in a shared store and balance freely.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"health-checks\">Health checks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Passive\u003c/strong> checks watch real traffic: after \u003ccode>max_fails\u003c/code> errors within a time, the server is skipped for \u003ccode>fail_timeout\u003c/code>. \u003cstrong>Active\u003c/strong> checks (HAProxy&#39;s \u003ccode>check\u003c/code>, nginx Plus or an external checker) probe an endpoint such as \u003ccode>/healthz\u003c/code> on a schedule. A good health endpoint checks the things the service needs (database, queue) but is cheap and does not fail because of a slow optional dependency.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"lb&gt;a;lb&gt;b;hc&gt;b;lb&gt;drain\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Balancing with health checks\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"83.3\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"lb\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Load balancer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"a\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Backend A\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"b\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Backend B\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"hc\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Health check\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"drain\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Drain\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Load balancer to Backend A; 2, Load balancer to Backend B; 3, Health check to Backend B; 4, Load balancer to Drain.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"lb\">\u003cdt>Load balancer \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Chooses a backend using the algorithm.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A bad algorithm for the traffic loads one server heavily.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"a\">\u003cdt>Backend A \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Healthy and in rotation.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If it fails, passive checks find out only after users see errors.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"b\">\u003cdt>Backend B \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Hung but listening; its health check fails.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A port-only check keeps sending it traffic.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"hc\">\u003cdt>Health check \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Probes a health endpoint on a schedule.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A shallow check says healthy when the service is broken.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"drain\">\u003cdt>Drain \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Stops new requests, lets current ones finish.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Killing a server mid-request drops user requests.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"changing-servers-safely\">Changing servers safely\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Before updating a backend, \u003cstrong>drain\u003c/strong> it: stop new requests and wait for current ones to finish. When adding a server, \u003cstrong>slow start\u003c/strong> ramps its share up so a cold cache does not get a full load at once. Limit retries to idempotent requests so a retry does not charge a card twice.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-one-in-four-requests-failed\">Incident: One in four requests failed\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A hung backend kept passing a TCP check and received a quarter of the traffic.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"for i in $(seq 20); do curl -s -o /dev/null -m 3 -w '%{http_code} ' https://lb.example.com/api; done\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> i \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> $(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">seq\u003c/span> 20); \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">do\u003c/span> curl -s -o /dev/null -m 3 -w \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;%{http_code} &#x27;\u003c/span> https://lb.example.com/api; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">done\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">200 200 504 200 200 200 504 200 200 504 200 200 ...\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">backend B: tcp check ok, /healthz timeout\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The load balancer only checked that port 8080 accepted a connection. Switching to an HTTP check of /healthz removed the hung server within seconds, and an alert now fires when any backend leaves rotation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Health checks that only test the TCP port\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sticky sessions to hide state kept in memory\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retrying non-idempotent requests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Removing a server without draining it\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"algorithms","text":"Algorithms"},{"id":"stickiness","text":"Stickiness"},{"id":"health-checks","text":"Health checks"},{"id":"changing-servers-safely","text":"Changing servers safely"},{"id":"incident-one-in-four-requests-failed","text":"Incident: One in four requests failed"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"nginx upstream module","u":"https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_upstream_module.html"},{"t":"HAProxy load balancing","u":"https://docs.haproxy.org/3.0/configuration.html#4.2-balance"}],"text":"Spreading requests is easy. Doing it well means choosing a method that fits your traffic and, more importantly, knowing when a backend is sick and taking it out of rotation quickly and safely. What you will learn Round robin, weighted, least connections and hashing Session stickiness and when to avoid it Passive and active health checks Draining, slow start and retries Algorithms upstream app_backend { least_conn; server 10.0.1.11:8080 weight= 2 max_fails= 3 fail_timeout= 15s ; server 10.0.1.12:8080 ; server 10.0.1.13:8080 backup; } Method Good for Round robin Equal servers and short, similar requests Weighted Servers of different size Least connections Long or uneven requests Hash on a key ( hash $request_uri consistent ) Caches, where the same key should reach the same server ip_hash Simple stickiness by client address Stickiness Sending a user to the same backend helps when sessions live in server memory, but it spreads load unevenly and a failed server loses its users&#39; sessions. Prefer to keep session data in a shared store and balance freely. Health checks Passive checks watch real traffic: after max_fails errors within a time, the server is skipped for fail_timeout . Active checks (HAProxy&#39;s check , nginx Plus or an external checker) probe an endpoint such as /healthz on a schedule. A good health endpoint checks the things the service needs (database, queue) but is cheap and does not fail because of a slow optional dependency. Balancing with health checks Flow in order: 1, Load balancer to Backend A; 2, Load balancer to Backend B; 3, Health check to Backend B; 4, Load balancer to Drain. Network edge Service Observability Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Load balancer Network edge What it does: Chooses a backend using the algorithm. If it fails or is weak: A bad algorithm for the traffic loads one server heavily. Backend A Service What it does: Healthy and in rotation. If it fails or is weak: If it fails, passive checks find out only after users see errors. Backend B Service What it does: Hung but listening; its health check fails. If it fails or is weak: A port-only check keeps sending it traffic. Health check Observability What it does: Probes a health endpoint on a schedule. If it fails or is weak: A shallow check says healthy when the service is broken. Drain Step What it does: Stops new requests, lets current ones finish. If it fails or is weak: Killing a server mid-request drops user requests. Changing servers safely Before updating a backend, drain it: stop new requests and wait for current ones to finish. When adding a server, slow start ramps its share up so a cold cache does not get a full load at once. Limit retries to idempotent requests so a retry does not charge a card twice. Incident: One in four requests failed Incident A hung backend kept passing a TCP check and received a quarter of the traffic. $ for i in $( seq 20); do curl -s -o /dev/null -m 3 -w &#x27;%{http_code} &#x27; https://lb.example.com/api; done 200 200 504 200 200 200 504 200 200 504 200 200 ... backend B: tcp check ok, /healthz timeout The load balancer only checked that port 8080 accepted a connection. Switching to an HTTP check of /healthz removed the hung server within seconds, and an alert now fires when any backend leaves rotation. Common mistakes Health checks that only test the TCP port Sticky sessions to hide state kept in memory Retrying non-idempotent requests Removing a server without draining it"},"proxies/timeouts-retries-keepalive-and-limits":{"html":"\u003cp>Most proxy outages are not crashes but timers: a read timeout shorter than a slow query, a retry storm against a struggling backend, or a keepalive that exhausts connections. Learning the timers and how they combine is the quickest way to stop guessing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The timeouts on each leg of a request\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keepalive to backends and to clients\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retries and why they can make things worse\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Connection and file-descriptor limits\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-timers\">The timers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"nginx\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"proxy_connect_timeout 3s;     # time to open a connection to the backend\nproxy_send_timeout    30s;    # gap between writes to the backend\nproxy_read_timeout    30s;    # gap between reads from the backend\nkeepalive_timeout     65s;    # idle client connection\nclient_body_timeout   15s;    # slow upload protection\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">proxy_connect_timeout\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3s\u003c/span>;     \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># time to open a connection to the backend\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">proxy_send_timeout\u003c/span>    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">30s\u003c/span>;    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># gap between writes to the backend\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">proxy_read_timeout\u003c/span>    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">30s\u003c/span>;    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># gap between reads from the backend\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">keepalive_timeout\u003c/span>     \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">65s\u003c/span>;    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># idle client connection\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">client_body_timeout\u003c/span>   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">15s\u003c/span>;    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># slow upload protection\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>proxy_read_timeout\u003c/code> is not the total time of a request; it is the maximum gap between two reads. A backend that sends nothing for 30 seconds is cut off even if the whole answer would have arrived at 31.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keepalive\">Keepalive\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Opening a new TCP (and TLS) connection per request is wasteful. Reuse backend connections:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"nginx\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"upstream app_backend { server 10.0.1.11:8080; keepalive 32; }\nlocation / { proxy_pass http://app_backend; proxy_http_version 1.1; proxy_set_header Connection &quot;&quot;; }\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">upstream\u003c/span> app_backend { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">server\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10.0.1.11:8080\u003c/span>; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">keepalive\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">32\u003c/span>; }\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">location\u003c/span> / { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">proxy_pass\u003c/span> http://app_backend; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">proxy_http_version\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>.\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">proxy_set_header\u003c/span> Connection \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&quot;\u003c/span>; }\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"retries\">Retries\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>proxy_next_upstream error timeout;\u003c/code> retries another server on those failures. Every retry multiplies load: with 3 attempts, a struggling cluster sees up to three times the requests exactly when it can least cope. Keep retries few, limit them to safe methods and add a total time budget (\u003ccode>proxy_next_upstream_timeout\u003c/code>).\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"user&gt;proxy;proxy&gt;app;app&gt;db;proxy&gt;retry\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Timeouts must nest\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"user\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">User or client\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"proxy\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Proxy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Application\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"db\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Database\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-queue\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"retry\" data-kind=\"queue\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Retries\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, User or client to Proxy; 2, Proxy to Application; 3, Application to Database; 4, Proxy to Retries.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-queue\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Queue\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"user\">\u003cdt>User or client \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Waits up to its own timeout, for example 60 seconds.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If it times out first, the proxy keeps working for nobody.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"proxy\">\u003cdt>Proxy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Has connect and read timeouts, for example 3 and 30 seconds.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A read timeout below the real response time cuts good requests.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>Application \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Calls a database with a 5 second timeout.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No timeout means a stuck thread holds a connection forever.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"db\">\u003cdt>Database \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Answers or fails within its own limit.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A slow query at the bottom becomes a 504 at the top.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"retry\">\u003cdt>Retries \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Send a failed request to another backend.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unbounded retries turn a slowdown into an outage.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"limits\">Limits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each connection uses a file descriptor. \u003ccode>worker_connections\u003c/code> times the number of workers caps concurrency, and each proxied request uses two connections (client and backend). Raise \u003ccode>worker_rlimit_nofile\u003c/code> and the OS limit together, and watch for &quot;worker_connections are not enough&quot; in the error log.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-report-that-always-timed-out\">Incident: The report that always timed out\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A report endpoint finished in 70 seconds but users always got a 504.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code} %{time_total}\\n' https://shop.example.com/reports/monthly\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s -o /dev/null -w \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;%{http_code} %{time_total}\\n&#x27;\u003c/span> https://shop.example.com/reports/monthly\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">504 60.003\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">error.log: upstream timed out (110: Connection timed out) while reading response header from upstream\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The proxy gave up at 60 seconds while the app was still working. A longer timeout would hide a design problem, so the team made the report asynchronous: a request creates a job and the page polls for the result.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating read timeout as the whole request time\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Setting the proxy timeout higher than the user&#39;s\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retrying without a limit or a budget\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Raising worker_connections without raising the OS file limit\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-timers","text":"The timers"},{"id":"keepalive","text":"Keepalive"},{"id":"retries","text":"Retries"},{"id":"limits","text":"Limits"},{"id":"incident-the-report-that-always-timed-out","text":"Incident: The report that always timed out"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"nginx proxy timeouts","u":"https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_proxy_module.html#proxy_read_timeout"},{"t":"HAProxy timeouts","u":"https://docs.haproxy.org/3.0/configuration.html#4.2-timeout%20connect"}],"text":"Most proxy outages are not crashes but timers: a read timeout shorter than a slow query, a retry storm against a struggling backend, or a keepalive that exhausts connections. Learning the timers and how they combine is the quickest way to stop guessing. What you will learn The timeouts on each leg of a request Keepalive to backends and to clients Retries and why they can make things worse Connection and file-descriptor limits The timers proxy_connect_timeout 3s ; # time to open a connection to the backend proxy_send_timeout 30s ; # gap between writes to the backend proxy_read_timeout 30s ; # gap between reads from the backend keepalive_timeout 65s ; # idle client connection client_body_timeout 15s ; # slow upload protection proxy_read_timeout is not the total time of a request; it is the maximum gap between two reads. A backend that sends nothing for 30 seconds is cut off even if the whole answer would have arrived at 31. Keepalive Opening a new TCP (and TLS) connection per request is wasteful. Reuse backend connections: upstream app_backend { server 10.0.1.11:8080 ; keepalive 32 ; } location / { proxy_pass http://app_backend; proxy_http_version 1 . 1 ; proxy_set_header Connection \"\" ; } Retries proxy_next_upstream error timeout; retries another server on those failures. Every retry multiplies load: with 3 attempts, a struggling cluster sees up to three times the requests exactly when it can least cope. Keep retries few, limit them to safe methods and add a total time budget ( proxy_next_upstream_timeout ). Timeouts must nest Flow in order: 1, User or client to Proxy; 2, Proxy to Application; 3, Application to Database; 4, Proxy to Retries. Client Network edge Service Data store Queue Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. User or client Client What it does: Waits up to its own timeout, for example 60 seconds. If it fails or is weak: If it times out first, the proxy keeps working for nobody. Proxy Network edge What it does: Has connect and read timeouts, for example 3 and 30 seconds. If it fails or is weak: A read timeout below the real response time cuts good requests. Application Service What it does: Calls a database with a 5 second timeout. If it fails or is weak: No timeout means a stuck thread holds a connection forever. Database Data store What it does: Answers or fails within its own limit. If it fails or is weak: A slow query at the bottom becomes a 504 at the top. Retries Queue What it does: Send a failed request to another backend. If it fails or is weak: Unbounded retries turn a slowdown into an outage. Limits Each connection uses a file descriptor. worker_connections times the number of workers caps concurrency, and each proxied request uses two connections (client and backend). Raise worker_rlimit_nofile and the OS limit together, and watch for \"worker_connections are not enough\" in the error log. Incident: The report that always timed out Incident A report endpoint finished in 70 seconds but users always got a 504. $ curl -s -o /dev/null -w &#x27;%{http_code} %{time_total}\\n&#x27; https://shop.example.com/reports/monthly 504 60.003 error.log: upstream timed out (110: Connection timed out) while reading response header from upstream The proxy gave up at 60 seconds while the app was still working. A longer timeout would hide a design problem, so the team made the report asynchronous: a request creates a job and the page polls for the result. Common mistakes Treating read timeout as the whole request time Setting the proxy timeout higher than the user&#39;s Retrying without a limit or a budget Raising worker_connections without raising the OS file limit"},"proxies/caching-compression-and-static-files":{"html":"\u003cp>The fastest request is one the backend never sees. A well-configured proxy cache can absorb most traffic for public content. A badly configured one leaks private pages. The difference is the cache key and the rules for what may be stored.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Serving static files with the right headers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>proxy_cache: key, validity, stale and locking\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What must never be cached\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Compression and its limits\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"static-files\">Static files\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"nginx\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"location /assets/ {\n    root /var/www/shop;\n    sendfile on;\n    expires 30d;\n    add_header Cache-Control &quot;public, immutable&quot;;\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">location\u003c/span> /assets/ {\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">root\u003c/span> /var/www/shop;\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">sendfile\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">on\u003c/span>;\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">expires\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">30d\u003c/span>;\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">add_header\u003c/span> Cache-Control \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;public, immutable&quot;\u003c/span>;\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Give assets a content hash in the file name (\u003ccode>app.3f9c1.js\u003c/code>) so they can be cached for a long time and replaced by changing the name, not by purging.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"caching-upstream-responses\">Caching upstream responses\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"nginx\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"proxy_cache_path /var/cache/nginx levels=1:2 keys_zone=api:50m max_size=2g inactive=60m;\nlocation /catalog/ {\n    proxy_cache api;\n    proxy_cache_key &quot;$scheme$host$request_uri&quot;;\n    proxy_cache_valid 200 301 5m;\n    proxy_cache_valid 404 1m;\n    proxy_cache_use_stale error timeout updating;\n    proxy_cache_lock on;\n    add_header X-Cache-Status $upstream_cache_status;\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">proxy_cache_path\u003c/span> /var/cache/nginx levels=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span> keys_zone=api:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">50m\u003c/span> max_size=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2g\u003c/span> inactive=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">60m\u003c/span>;\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">location\u003c/span> /catalog/ {\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">proxy_cache\u003c/span> api;\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">proxy_cache_key\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$scheme\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$host\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$request_uri\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>;\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">proxy_cache_valid\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">200\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">301\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5m\u003c/span>;\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">proxy_cache_valid\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">404\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1m\u003c/span>;\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">proxy_cache_use_stale\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">error\u003c/span> timeout updating;\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">proxy_cache_lock\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">on\u003c/span>;\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">add_header\u003c/span> X-Cache-Status \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$upstream_cache_status\u003c/span>;\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>key\u003c/strong> decides what counts as the same request. \u003ccode>proxy_cache_lock\u003c/code> makes concurrent requests for a missing item wait for one backend fetch, avoiding a stampede. \u003ccode>use_stale\u003c/code> serves an old copy while the backend is failing, which turns an outage into a slow patch. The header shows HIT, MISS, EXPIRED or STALE for debugging.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-not-to-cache\">What not to cache\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Anything personalised (cookies, authorisation headers, account pages) must bypass the cache or vary the key by user. nginx does not cache responses with \u003ccode>Set-Cookie\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>Cache-Control: private\u003c/code> by default; leave those protections on.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"user&gt;cache;cache&gt;app;cache&gt;stale;cache&gt;gz\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A cached request\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"user\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">User\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-cache\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cache\" data-kind=\"cache\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Proxy cache\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Cache\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Backend\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"stale\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Stale copy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"gz\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Compression\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, User to Proxy cache; 2, Proxy cache to Backend; 3, Proxy cache to Stale copy; 4, Proxy cache to Compression.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-cache\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Cache\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"user\">\u003cdt>User \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Asks for a public catalogue page.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A personalised page cached here leaks one user's data.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cache\">\u003cdt>Proxy cache \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Cache\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Answers HIT from disk, or MISS and asks the backend.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A key that ignores a header or cookie mixes up different users.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>Backend \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Builds the page only on a MISS.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Without cache lock, a popular expiry sends a stampede here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"stale\">\u003cdt>Stale copy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Served if the backend is slow or down.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Stale content can mislead; keep the window short.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"gz\">\u003cdt>Compression \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Shrinks text responses before sending.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Compressing already-compressed files wastes CPU.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"compression\">Compression\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>gzip on; gzip_types text/css application/json application/javascript;\u003c/code> shrinks text by 70 percent or more. Do not compress images or video, which are already compressed, and keep levels modest (4 to 6): higher levels cost CPU for little gain.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-everyone-saw-the-same-name\">Incident: Everyone saw the same name\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A cached page that showed a user&#39;s name was served to others.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"curl -s -H 'Cookie: sid=bob' https://shop.example.com/home | grep Hello\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s -H \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Cookie: sid=bob&#x27;\u003c/span> https://shop.example.com/home | grep Hello\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Hello Alice\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">X-Cache-Status: HIT\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The cache key was only the URL. The team bypassed the cache when a session cookie was present, moved the personal part of the page to a separate uncached request and added a test that fetches the page as two users.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A cache key that ignores user-specific headers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Long cache lifetimes on files with no content hash\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Caching error responses for too long\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Compressing images and archives\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"static-files","text":"Static files"},{"id":"caching-upstream-responses","text":"Caching upstream responses"},{"id":"what-not-to-cache","text":"What not to cache"},{"id":"compression","text":"Compression"},{"id":"incident-everyone-saw-the-same-name","text":"Incident: Everyone saw the same name"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"nginx content caching","u":"https://docs.nginx.com/nginx/admin-guide/content-cache/content-caching/"},{"t":"HTTP caching (MDN)","u":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Caching"}],"text":"The fastest request is one the backend never sees. A well-configured proxy cache can absorb most traffic for public content. A badly configured one leaks private pages. The difference is the cache key and the rules for what may be stored. What you will learn Serving static files with the right headers proxy_cache: key, validity, stale and locking What must never be cached Compression and its limits Static files location /assets/ { root /var/www/shop; sendfile on ; expires 30d ; add_header Cache-Control \"public, immutable\" ; } Give assets a content hash in the file name ( app.3f9c1.js ) so they can be cached for a long time and replaced by changing the name, not by purging. Caching upstream responses proxy_cache_path /var/cache/nginx levels= 1 : 2 keys_zone=api: 50m max_size= 2g inactive= 60m ; location /catalog/ { proxy_cache api; proxy_cache_key \" $scheme $host $request_uri \" ; proxy_cache_valid 200 301 5m ; proxy_cache_valid 404 1m ; proxy_cache_use_stale error timeout updating; proxy_cache_lock on ; add_header X-Cache-Status $upstream_cache_status ; } The key decides what counts as the same request. proxy_cache_lock makes concurrent requests for a missing item wait for one backend fetch, avoiding a stampede. use_stale serves an old copy while the backend is failing, which turns an outage into a slow patch. The header shows HIT, MISS, EXPIRED or STALE for debugging. What not to cache Anything personalised (cookies, authorisation headers, account pages) must bypass the cache or vary the key by user. nginx does not cache responses with Set-Cookie or Cache-Control: private by default; leave those protections on. A cached request Flow in order: 1, User to Proxy cache; 2, Proxy cache to Backend; 3, Proxy cache to Stale copy; 4, Proxy cache to Compression. Client Cache Service Data store Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. User Client What it does: Asks for a public catalogue page. If it fails or is weak: A personalised page cached here leaks one user's data. Proxy cache Cache What it does: Answers HIT from disk, or MISS and asks the backend. If it fails or is weak: A key that ignores a header or cookie mixes up different users. Backend Service What it does: Builds the page only on a MISS. If it fails or is weak: Without cache lock, a popular expiry sends a stampede here. Stale copy Data store What it does: Served if the backend is slow or down. If it fails or is weak: Stale content can mislead; keep the window short. Compression Step What it does: Shrinks text responses before sending. If it fails or is weak: Compressing already-compressed files wastes CPU. Compression gzip on; gzip_types text/css application/json application/javascript; shrinks text by 70 percent or more. Do not compress images or video, which are already compressed, and keep levels modest (4 to 6): higher levels cost CPU for little gain. Incident: Everyone saw the same name Incident A cached page that showed a user&#39;s name was served to others. $ curl -s -H &#x27;Cookie: sid=bob&#x27; https://shop.example.com/home | grep Hello Hello Alice X-Cache-Status: HIT The cache key was only the URL. The team bypassed the cache when a session cookie was present, moved the personal part of the page to a separate uncached request and added a test that fetches the page as two users. Common mistakes A cache key that ignores user-specific headers Long cache lifetimes on files with no content hash Caching error responses for too long Compressing images and archives"},"proxies/rate-limiting-and-protecting-backends":{"html":"\u003cp>Backends rarely fail from steady load; they fail from bursts and bugs. The proxy is the cheapest place to cap them, before a request costs a database query. A good limit is fair, visible and kind to well-behaved clients.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How the leaky bucket, burst and nodelay work\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing the key: address, API key or token\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Returning 429 and Retry-After\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Limiting connections, body size and slow clients\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-request-limit\">A request limit\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"nginx\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=perip:10m rate=10r/s;\nlimit_req_status 429;\n\nlocation /api/ {\n    limit_req zone=perip burst=20 nodelay;\n    proxy_pass http://app_backend;\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">limit_req_zone\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$binary_remote_addr\u003c/span> zone=perip:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10m\u003c/span> rate=10r/s;\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">limit_req_status\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">429\u003c/span>;\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">location\u003c/span> /api/ {\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">limit_req\u003c/span> zone=perip burst=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">20\u003c/span> nodelay;\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">proxy_pass\u003c/span> http://app_backend;\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>rate=10r/s\u003c/code> fills the bucket at ten requests a second. \u003ccode>burst=20\u003c/code> lets a client go 20 requests over the rate before being refused; \u003ccode>nodelay\u003c/code> serves the burst immediately instead of spacing it out. Requests beyond are refused with 429.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"who-is-counted\">Who is counted\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The key decides fairness. \u003ccode>$binary_remote_addr\u003c/code> counts per client address, which is wrong behind a CDN or NAT (many users, one address, or the CDN&#39;s address for everyone). Behind a CDN use the real-client header, validated from trusted proxies only. For APIs, key on an API key or token (\u003ccode>$http_x_api_key\u003c/code>) so each customer has a budget.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"other-limits\">Other limits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"nginx\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"limit_conn_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=conn:10m;\nlimit_conn conn 20;           # at most 20 parallel connections per client\nclient_max_body_size 5m;      # large uploads rejected with 413\nclient_body_timeout 10s;      # slow uploads closed\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">limit_conn_zone\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$binary_remote_addr\u003c/span> zone=conn:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10m\u003c/span>;\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">limit_conn\u003c/span> conn \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">20\u003c/span>;           \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># at most 20 parallel connections per client\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">client_max_body_size\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5m\u003c/span>;      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># large uploads rejected with 413\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">client_body_timeout\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10s\u003c/span>;      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># slow uploads closed\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Tell clients what happened: \u003ccode>429 Too Many Requests\u003c/code> with a \u003ccode>Retry-After\u003c/code> header lets well-written clients back off. Exempt health checks and internal addresses so monitoring does not get limited.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"client&gt;limit;limit&gt;conn;conn&gt;app;limit&gt;mon\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Where limits protect the backend\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"client\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Clients\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"limit\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Rate limit\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"conn\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Connection and body caps\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Backend\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"mon\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Metrics\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Clients to Rate limit; 2, Rate limit to Connection and body caps; 3, Connection and body caps to Backend; 4, Rate limit to Metrics.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"client\">\u003cdt>Clients \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Real users and the occasional buggy or abusive one.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> One client in a retry loop can look like an attack.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"limit\">\u003cdt>Rate limit \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Counts per key and refuses with 429 above the budget.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> The wrong key limits innocent users sharing an address.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"conn\">\u003cdt>Connection and body caps \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Cap parallel connections, upload size and slow senders.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Without them, slow clients tie up workers.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>Backend \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Sees only the traffic the proxy lets through.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unprotected, a burst reaches the database.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"mon\">\u003cdt>Metrics \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Count 429s per key and alert on spikes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Silent limits hide a client bug or an attack.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-retry-loop\">Incident: The retry loop\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new app version retried failures instantly and flooded the API.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">access.log: 5200 req/s, 3900 distinct clients\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">upstream: 502 rate 41 percent\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">db: connections 100/100\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The bug amplified every failure into more requests. A per-client limit with burst, 429 with Retry-After, and exponential backoff in the app stopped the loop and let the API recover.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Limiting by address behind a CDN where every request has the CDN&#39;s address\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rate limits with no metrics or alerts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Limiting health checks and monitors\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No Retry-After so clients retry at once\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"a-request-limit","text":"A request limit"},{"id":"who-is-counted","text":"Who is counted"},{"id":"other-limits","text":"Other limits"},{"id":"incident-the-retry-loop","text":"Incident: The retry loop"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Rate limiting with nginx","u":"https://blog.nginx.org/blog/rate-limiting-nginx"},{"t":"ngx_http_limit_req_module","u":"https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_limit_req_module.html"}],"text":"Backends rarely fail from steady load; they fail from bursts and bugs. The proxy is the cheapest place to cap them, before a request costs a database query. A good limit is fair, visible and kind to well-behaved clients. What you will learn How the leaky bucket, burst and nodelay work Choosing the key: address, API key or token Returning 429 and Retry-After Limiting connections, body size and slow clients A request limit limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=perip: 10m rate=10r/s; limit_req_status 429 ; location /api/ { limit_req zone=perip burst= 20 nodelay; proxy_pass http://app_backend; } rate=10r/s fills the bucket at ten requests a second. burst=20 lets a client go 20 requests over the rate before being refused; nodelay serves the burst immediately instead of spacing it out. Requests beyond are refused with 429. Who is counted The key decides fairness. $binary_remote_addr counts per client address, which is wrong behind a CDN or NAT (many users, one address, or the CDN&#39;s address for everyone). Behind a CDN use the real-client header, validated from trusted proxies only. For APIs, key on an API key or token ( $http_x_api_key ) so each customer has a budget. Other limits limit_conn_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=conn: 10m ; limit_conn conn 20 ; # at most 20 parallel connections per client client_max_body_size 5m ; # large uploads rejected with 413 client_body_timeout 10s ; # slow uploads closed Tell clients what happened: 429 Too Many Requests with a Retry-After header lets well-written clients back off. Exempt health checks and internal addresses so monitoring does not get limited. Where limits protect the backend Flow in order: 1, Clients to Rate limit; 2, Rate limit to Connection and body caps; 3, Connection and body caps to Backend; 4, Rate limit to Metrics. Client Security Step Service Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Clients Client What it does: Real users and the occasional buggy or abusive one. If it fails or is weak: One client in a retry loop can look like an attack. Rate limit Security What it does: Counts per key and refuses with 429 above the budget. If it fails or is weak: The wrong key limits innocent users sharing an address. Connection and body caps Step What it does: Cap parallel connections, upload size and slow senders. If it fails or is weak: Without them, slow clients tie up workers. Backend Service What it does: Sees only the traffic the proxy lets through. If it fails or is weak: Unprotected, a burst reaches the database. Metrics Observability What it does: Count 429s per key and alert on spikes. If it fails or is weak: Silent limits hide a client bug or an attack. Incident: The retry loop Incident A new app version retried failures instantly and flooded the API. access.log: 5200 req/s, 3900 distinct clients upstream: 502 rate 41 percent db: connections 100/100 The bug amplified every failure into more requests. A per-client limit with burst, 429 with Retry-After, and exponential backoff in the app stopped the loop and let the API recover. Common mistakes Limiting by address behind a CDN where every request has the CDN&#39;s address Rate limits with no metrics or alerts Limiting health checks and monitors No Retry-After so clients retry at once"},"proxies/haproxy-frontends-backends-and-acls":{"html":"\u003cp>HAProxy is built for balancing. Its configuration is explicit and its runtime tooling is excellent. Learning its four section types and how ACLs route lets you read any HAProxy file.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The sections of an HAProxy configuration\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>ACLs and routing with use_backend\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Health checks, rise and fall\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The stats page and runtime socket\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sections\">Sections\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"haproxy\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"global\n    maxconn 20000\n    log /dev/log local0\ndefaults\n    mode http\n    timeout connect 3s\n    timeout client  30s\n    timeout server  30s\n    option httplog\nfrontend web\n    bind :443 ssl crt /etc/haproxy/certs/\n    acl is_api path_beg /api/\n    acl is_admin hdr(host) -i admin.example.com\n    use_backend api  if is_api\n    use_backend admin if is_admin\n    default_backend site\nbackend site\n    balance roundrobin\n    option httpchk GET /healthz\n    server s1 10.0.1.11:8080 check inter 2s rise 2 fall 3\n    server s2 10.0.1.12:8080 check inter 2s rise 2 fall 3\nbackend api\n    balance leastconn\n    server a1 10.0.2.11:9000 check\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">global\n    maxconn 20000\n    log /dev/log local0\ndefaults\n    mode http\n    timeout connect 3s\n    timeout client  30s\n    timeout server  30s\n    option httplog\nfrontend web\n    bind :443 ssl crt /etc/haproxy/certs/\n    acl is_api path_beg /api/\n    acl is_admin hdr(host) -i admin.example.com\n    use_backend api  if is_api\n    use_backend admin if is_admin\n    default_backend site\nbackend site\n    balance roundrobin\n    option httpchk GET /healthz\n    server s1 10.0.1.11:8080 check inter 2s rise 2 fall 3\n    server s2 10.0.1.12:8080 check inter 2s rise 2 fall 3\nbackend api\n    balance leastconn\n    server a1 10.0.2.11:9000 check\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>global\u003c/code> is process-wide, \u003ccode>defaults\u003c/code> sets shared values, \u003ccode>frontend\u003c/code> accepts connections and \u003ccode>backend\u003c/code> holds servers. An \u003cstrong>ACL\u003c/strong> is a named test (path starts with /api/, host equals admin.example.com); \u003ccode>use_backend ... if\u003c/code> applies it. \u003ccode>rise 2 fall 3\u003c/code> means two good checks to enter rotation and three bad ones to leave, which avoids flapping.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"live-state\">Live state\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The stats page shows each server&#39;s status, sessions, queue and error counts. The \u003cstrong>runtime socket\u003c/strong> lets you change state without a reload:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"echo &quot;set server site/s1 state drain&quot; | socat stdio /run/haproxy/admin.sock\necho &quot;show servers state site&quot; | socat stdio /run/haproxy/admin.sock\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;set server site/s1 state drain&quot;\u003c/span> | socat stdio /run/haproxy/admin.sock\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;show servers state site&quot;\u003c/span> | socat stdio /run/haproxy/admin.sock\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Stick tables\u003c/strong> store per-client counters (requests, errors) in memory, usable for rate limiting and stickiness.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"bind&gt;acl;acl&gt;be;be&gt;srv;be&gt;stats\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How HAProxy routes a request\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"bind\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Frontend\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"acl\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">ACLs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"be\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Backend\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"srv\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Server\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"stats\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Stats and runtime socket\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Frontend to ACLs; 2, ACLs to Backend; 3, Backend to Server; 4, Backend to Stats and runtime socket.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"bind\">\u003cdt>Frontend \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Accepts the connection and terminates TLS.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A wrong bind or certificate path stops all traffic.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"acl\">\u003cdt>ACLs \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Name tests on host, path, header or source.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An ACL that is too broad sends traffic to the wrong backend.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"be\">\u003cdt>Backend \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Chooses a server using the balance method and health.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If all servers are down, requests get 503.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"srv\">\u003cdt>Server \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Answers the request.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A slow server fills the queue; watch queue depth.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"stats\">\u003cdt>Stats and runtime socket \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Show live status and allow drain without reload.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Expose it only to admins; it can change state.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-503-on-every-request\">Incident: The 503 on every request\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After a deploy all servers failed health checks and HAProxy answered 503 for every request.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"echo &quot;show servers state site&quot; | socat stdio /run/haproxy/admin.sock\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;show servers state site&quot;\u003c/span> | socat stdio /run/haproxy/admin.sock\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">site s1 10.0.1.11 srv_op_state=0 (L7STS 404)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">site s2 10.0.1.12 srv_op_state=0 (L7STS 404)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">haproxy: backend site has no server available!\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The new release moved the health endpoint from /health to /healthz, so the check got 404 and marked every server down. The fix was to update the check path, and the team added the health path to the release checklist and a canary server.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Health check path that differs from the real endpoint\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Setting fall to 1, which causes flapping\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Exposing the admin socket or stats page publicly\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reloading for a change the runtime socket can make\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"sections","text":"Sections"},{"id":"live-state","text":"Live state"},{"id":"incident-the-503-on-every-request","text":"Incident: The 503 on every request"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"HAProxy configuration manual","u":"https://docs.haproxy.org/3.0/configuration.html"},{"t":"HAProxy management guide","u":"https://docs.haproxy.org/3.0/management.html"}],"text":"HAProxy is built for balancing. Its configuration is explicit and its runtime tooling is excellent. Learning its four section types and how ACLs route lets you read any HAProxy file. What you will learn The sections of an HAProxy configuration ACLs and routing with use_backend Health checks, rise and fall The stats page and runtime socket Sections global maxconn 20000 log /dev/log local0 defaults mode http timeout connect 3s timeout client 30s timeout server 30s option httplog frontend web bind :443 ssl crt /etc/haproxy/certs/ acl is_api path_beg /api/ acl is_admin hdr(host) -i admin.example.com use_backend api if is_api use_backend admin if is_admin default_backend site backend site balance roundrobin option httpchk GET /healthz server s1 10.0.1.11:8080 check inter 2s rise 2 fall 3 server s2 10.0.1.12:8080 check inter 2s rise 2 fall 3 backend api balance leastconn server a1 10.0.2.11:9000 check global is process-wide, defaults sets shared values, frontend accepts connections and backend holds servers. An ACL is a named test (path starts with /api/, host equals admin.example.com); use_backend ... if applies it. rise 2 fall 3 means two good checks to enter rotation and three bad ones to leave, which avoids flapping. Live state The stats page shows each server&#39;s status, sessions, queue and error counts. The runtime socket lets you change state without a reload: $ echo \"set server site/s1 state drain\" | socat stdio /run/haproxy/admin.sock $ echo \"show servers state site\" | socat stdio /run/haproxy/admin.sock Stick tables store per-client counters (requests, errors) in memory, usable for rate limiting and stickiness. How HAProxy routes a request Flow in order: 1, Frontend to ACLs; 2, ACLs to Backend; 3, Backend to Server; 4, Backend to Stats and runtime socket. Service Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Frontend Service What it does: Accepts the connection and terminates TLS. If it fails or is weak: A wrong bind or certificate path stops all traffic. ACLs Step What it does: Name tests on host, path, header or source. If it fails or is weak: An ACL that is too broad sends traffic to the wrong backend. Backend Service What it does: Chooses a server using the balance method and health. If it fails or is weak: If all servers are down, requests get 503. Server Service What it does: Answers the request. If it fails or is weak: A slow server fills the queue; watch queue depth. Stats and runtime socket Observability What it does: Show live status and allow drain without reload. If it fails or is weak: Expose it only to admins; it can change state. Incident: The 503 on every request Incident After a deploy all servers failed health checks and HAProxy answered 503 for every request. $ echo \"show servers state site\" | socat stdio /run/haproxy/admin.sock site s1 10.0.1.11 srv_op_state=0 (L7STS 404) site s2 10.0.1.12 srv_op_state=0 (L7STS 404) haproxy: backend site has no server available! The new release moved the health endpoint from /health to /healthz, so the check got 404 and marked every server down. The fix was to update the check path, and the team added the health path to the release checklist and a canary server. Common mistakes Health check path that differs from the real endpoint Setting fall to 1, which causes flapping Exposing the admin socket or stats page publicly Reloading for a change the runtime socket can make"},"proxies/envoy-traefik-and-dynamic-proxies":{"html":"\u003cp>In containers and Kubernetes, backends appear and disappear all day. Static files and reloads work, but several proxies were built to follow the platform instead. They differ in complexity, features and who they suit.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Envoy: listeners, routes, clusters and endpoints\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Traefik: entrypoints, routers, services and providers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Caddy: automatic HTTPS and a short config\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to choose between nginx, HAProxy, Envoy, Traefik and Caddy\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"envoy\">Envoy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Envoy is a high-performance proxy with rich observability, used as the data plane in many service meshes and gateways. Its model: a \u003cstrong>listener\u003c/strong> accepts connections, \u003cstrong>filters\u003c/strong> (such as the HTTP connection manager) process them, \u003cstrong>routes\u003c/strong> map requests to a \u003cstrong>cluster\u003c/strong>, and a cluster holds \u003cstrong>endpoints\u003c/strong>. The control plane sends updates over \u003cstrong>xDS\u003c/strong> APIs, so routes and endpoints change live.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"static_resources:\n  listeners:\n  - name: main\n    address: { socket_address: { address: 0.0.0.0, port_value: 8080 } }\n    filter_chains:\n    - filters:\n      - name: envoy.filters.network.http_connection_manager\n        typed_config:\n          &quot;@type&quot;: type.googleapis.com/envoy.extensions.filters.network.http_connection_manager.v3.HttpConnectionManager\n          stat_prefix: ingress\n          route_config:\n            virtual_hosts:\n            - name: all\n              domains: [&quot;*&quot;]\n              routes:\n              - match: { prefix: &quot;/api&quot; }\n                route: { cluster: api }\n          http_filters:\n          - name: envoy.filters.http.router\n            typed_config: { &quot;@type&quot;: type.googleapis.com/envoy.extensions.filters.http.router.v3.Router }\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">static_resources:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">listeners:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">main\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">address:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">socket_address:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">address:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">.0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">.0\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">port_value:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8080\u003c/span> } }\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">filter_chains:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">filters:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">envoy.filters.network.http_connection_manager\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">typed_config:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;@type&quot;:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">type.googleapis.com/envoy.extensions.filters.network.http_connection_manager.v3.HttpConnectionManager\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stat_prefix:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ingress\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">route_config:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">virtual_hosts:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">all\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">domains:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;*&quot;\u003c/span>]\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">routes:\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">match:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">prefix:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/api&quot;\u003c/span> }\n                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">route:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cluster:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">api\u003c/span> }\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">http_filters:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">envoy.filters.http.router\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">typed_config:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;@type&quot;:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">type.googleapis.com/envoy.extensions.filters.http.router.v3.Router\u003c/span> }\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"traefik\">Traefik\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Traefik discovers services from \u003cstrong>providers\u003c/strong> (Docker labels, Kubernetes Ingress or IngressRoute, files). An \u003cstrong>entrypoint\u003c/strong> is a port, a \u003cstrong>router\u003c/strong> matches rules (\u003ccode>Host(...)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>PathPrefix(...)\u003c/code>), and a \u003cstrong>service\u003c/strong> lists the servers. Middleware adds rate limits, auth and redirects.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"labels:\n  - &quot;traefik.http.routers.shop.rule=Host(`shop.example.com`)&quot;\n  - &quot;traefik.http.routers.shop.tls.certresolver=letsencrypt&quot;\n  - &quot;traefik.http.services.shop.loadbalancer.server.port=8080&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">labels:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;traefik.http.routers.shop.rule=Host(`shop.example.com`)&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;traefik.http.routers.shop.tls.certresolver=letsencrypt&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;traefik.http.services.shop.loadbalancer.server.port=8080&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"caddy\">Caddy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Caddy obtains and renews HTTPS certificates by default and its config is short:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"shop.example.com {\n    reverse_proxy 10.0.1.11:8080 10.0.1.12:8080\n    encode gzip\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">shop.example.com {\n    reverse_proxy 10.0.1.11:8080 10.0.1.12:8080\n    encode gzip\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"choosing\">Choosing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>nginx: very common, great for web serving and caching. HAProxy: excellent load balancing and live state. Envoy: service meshes, gRPC, deep metrics. Traefik: containers and Kubernetes with automatic discovery. Caddy: small deployments and simple HTTPS.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"plat&gt;disc;disc&gt;proxy;proxy&gt;svc;proxy&gt;obs\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A dynamic proxy follows the platform\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"plat\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Platform\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"disc\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Discovery\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"proxy\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Proxy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"svc\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Services\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"obs\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Metrics and access logs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Platform to Discovery; 2, Discovery to Proxy; 3, Proxy to Services; 4, Proxy to Metrics and access logs.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"plat\">\u003cdt>Platform \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Containers or pods start and stop; labels or resources describe them.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong labels mean a service is invisible to the proxy.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"disc\">\u003cdt>Discovery \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The provider or xDS control plane watches for changes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A control plane outage freezes routes at the last known state.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"proxy\">\u003cdt>Proxy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Updates routes live without a reload.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A bad update can send traffic nowhere; validate before applying.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"svc\">\u003cdt>Services \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Receive traffic as soon as they are ready.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Routing before readiness gives errors during a rollout.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"obs\">\u003cdt>Metrics and access logs \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Show routes, latency and errors per service.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Without them, a dynamic proxy is a black box.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-route-nobody-could-see\">Incident: The route nobody could see\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new service never received traffic because its label had a typo.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\\n' https://new.example.com\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s -o /dev/null -w \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;%{http_code}\\n&#x27;\u003c/span> https://new.example.com\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">404\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">traefik dashboard: no router matches host new.example.com\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">label: traefik.http.routers.neww.rule  (typo: neww)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The label mentioned a router name that did not match the service definition, so no route existed. The team added a dashboard check to the deploy pipeline and a smoke test that requests the new host.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Applying dynamic configuration with no validation or smoke test\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Exposing the proxy dashboard or admin API publicly\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using Envoy for a simple site where nginx or Caddy is enough\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not monitoring the control plane that feeds the proxy\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"envoy","text":"Envoy"},{"id":"traefik","text":"Traefik"},{"id":"caddy","text":"Caddy"},{"id":"choosing","text":"Choosing"},{"id":"incident-the-route-nobody-could-see","text":"Incident: The route nobody could see"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Envoy architecture overview","u":"https://www.envoyproxy.io/docs/envoy/latest/intro/arch_overview/arch_overview"},{"t":"Traefik documentation","u":"https://doc.traefik.io/traefik/"},{"t":"Caddy documentation","u":"https://caddyserver.com/docs/"}],"text":"In containers and Kubernetes, backends appear and disappear all day. Static files and reloads work, but several proxies were built to follow the platform instead. They differ in complexity, features and who they suit. What you will learn Envoy: listeners, routes, clusters and endpoints Traefik: entrypoints, routers, services and providers Caddy: automatic HTTPS and a short config How to choose between nginx, HAProxy, Envoy, Traefik and Caddy Envoy Envoy is a high-performance proxy with rich observability, used as the data plane in many service meshes and gateways. Its model: a listener accepts connections, filters (such as the HTTP connection manager) process them, routes map requests to a cluster , and a cluster holds endpoints . The control plane sends updates over xDS APIs, so routes and endpoints change live. static_resources: listeners: - name: main address: { socket_address: { address: 0.0 .0 .0 , port_value: 8080 } } filter_chains: - filters: - name: envoy.filters.network.http_connection_manager typed_config: \"@type\": type.googleapis.com/envoy.extensions.filters.network.http_connection_manager.v3.HttpConnectionManager stat_prefix: ingress route_config: virtual_hosts: - name: all domains: [ \"*\" ] routes: - match: { prefix: \"/api\" } route: { cluster: api } http_filters: - name: envoy.filters.http.router typed_config: { \"@type\": type.googleapis.com/envoy.extensions.filters.http.router.v3.Router } Traefik Traefik discovers services from providers (Docker labels, Kubernetes Ingress or IngressRoute, files). An entrypoint is a port, a router matches rules ( Host(...) , PathPrefix(...) ), and a service lists the servers. Middleware adds rate limits, auth and redirects. labels: - \"traefik.http.routers.shop.rule=Host(`shop.example.com`)\" - \"traefik.http.routers.shop.tls.certresolver=letsencrypt\" - \"traefik.http.services.shop.loadbalancer.server.port=8080\" Caddy Caddy obtains and renews HTTPS certificates by default and its config is short: shop.example.com { reverse_proxy 10.0.1.11:8080 10.0.1.12:8080 encode gzip } Choosing nginx: very common, great for web serving and caching. HAProxy: excellent load balancing and live state. Envoy: service meshes, gRPC, deep metrics. Traefik: containers and Kubernetes with automatic discovery. Caddy: small deployments and simple HTTPS. A dynamic proxy follows the platform Flow in order: 1, Platform to Discovery; 2, Discovery to Proxy; 3, Proxy to Services; 4, Proxy to Metrics and access logs. Step Network edge Service Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Platform Step What it does: Containers or pods start and stop; labels or resources describe them. If it fails or is weak: Wrong labels mean a service is invisible to the proxy. Discovery Step What it does: The provider or xDS control plane watches for changes. If it fails or is weak: A control plane outage freezes routes at the last known state. Proxy Network edge What it does: Updates routes live without a reload. If it fails or is weak: A bad update can send traffic nowhere; validate before applying. Services Service What it does: Receive traffic as soon as they are ready. If it fails or is weak: Routing before readiness gives errors during a rollout. Metrics and access logs Observability What it does: Show routes, latency and errors per service. If it fails or is weak: Without them, a dynamic proxy is a black box. Incident: The route nobody could see Incident A new service never received traffic because its label had a typo. $ curl -s -o /dev/null -w &#x27;%{http_code}\\n&#x27; https://new.example.com 404 traefik dashboard: no router matches host new.example.com label: traefik.http.routers.neww.rule (typo: neww) The label mentioned a router name that did not match the service definition, so no route existed. The team added a dashboard check to the deploy pipeline and a smoke test that requests the new host. Common mistakes Applying dynamic configuration"},"proxies/zero-downtime-reloads-and-blue-green-at-the-proxy":{"html":"\u003cp>The front door should never be closed for maintenance. Reloads, weights and drains let you change both the proxy and the services behind it while users keep working.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What a graceful reload does\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Blue-green switching and rollback\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Canary routing by weight, header or cookie\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Draining and readiness during deploys\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"graceful-reload\">Graceful reload\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>nginx -s reload\u003c/code> (or \u003ccode>systemctl reload nginx\u003c/code>) parses the new config. If it is valid, new workers start with it and old workers stop accepting new connections and exit when their current requests finish. If the new config is invalid, the old workers keep running. A \u003cstrong>restart\u003c/strong> would drop everything, so use reload for changes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"blue-green\">Blue-green\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run two complete environments, blue (live) and green (new). Test green privately, then switch the proxy&#39;s upstream in one reload. Rollback is switching back.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"nginx\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"upstream app_live { server 10.0.1.11:8080; server 10.0.1.12:8080; }  # blue\nupstream app_next { server 10.0.2.11:8080; server 10.0.2.12:8080; }  # green\nmap $http_x_canary $pool { default app_live; &quot;1&quot; app_next; }\nserver { location / { proxy_pass http://$pool; } }\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">upstream\u003c/span> app_live { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">server\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10.0.1.11:8080\u003c/span>; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">server\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10.0.1.12:8080\u003c/span>; }  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># blue\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">upstream\u003c/span> app_next { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">server\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10.0.2.11:8080\u003c/span>; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">server\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10.0.2.12:8080\u003c/span>; }  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># green\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">map\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$http_x_canary\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$pool\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">default\u003c/span> app_live; &quot;1&quot; app_next; }\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">server\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">location\u003c/span> / { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">proxy_pass\u003c/span> http://\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$pool\u003c/span>; } }\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The header route lets testers reach green through the real proxy before anyone else does.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"canary-by-weight\">Canary by weight\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"nginx\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"split_clients &quot;${remote_addr}${http_user_agent}&quot; $pool {\n    5%  app_next;\n    *   app_live;\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">split_clients\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${remote_addr}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${http_user_agent}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$pool\u003c/span> {\n    5%  app_next;\n    *   app_live;\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Send 5 percent to the new version, watch error rate and latency, then 25, 50 and 100. Roll back by setting the share to zero.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"proxy&gt;live;proxy&gt;next;proxy&gt;watch;watch&gt;back\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Shifting traffic safely\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"16.7\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"16.7\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"83.3\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"live\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Blue (live)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"next\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Green (new)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"proxy\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Proxy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"watch\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Error rate and latency\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"back\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Rollback\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Proxy to Blue (live); 2, Proxy to Green (new); 3, Proxy to Error rate and latency; 4, Error rate and latency to Rollback.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"live\">\u003cdt>Blue (live) \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The current version serving everyone.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> It must stay healthy until the new version is proven.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"next\">\u003cdt>Green (new) \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The new version, tested privately first.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An untested green receives real users at the switch.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"proxy\">\u003cdt>Proxy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A reload switches or weights the upstream.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Restarting instead of reloading drops connections.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"watch\">\u003cdt>Error rate and latency \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Compare the new version with the old at each step.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No comparison means no signal to roll back.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"back\">\u003cdt>Rollback \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Return the share to blue in one reload.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A rollback that was never tested may fail when needed.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"drain-and-readiness\">Drain and readiness\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Remove a server from rotation, wait for current requests to finish, then stop it. A new server should join only after its readiness check passes. In Kubernetes this is what readiness probes and preStop hooks do for you; on plain hosts you do it with the proxy&#39;s drain or weight controls.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-restart-that-dropped-uploads\">Incident: The restart that dropped uploads\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A config change applied with a service restart cut every open connection during business hours.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"systemctl restart nginx\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>systemctl restart nginx\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">access.log: 1042 requests ended with status 499\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">client: net::ERR_CONNECTION_RESET\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Users lost uploads in progress. The team switched to nginx -t and reload for every change, and moved risky changes to a canary share first. Restart is reserved for upgrades, and happens only after drain.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Restarting instead of reloading\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Switching all traffic at once with no way back\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Stopping a server before its requests finish\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sending traffic to a server before it is ready\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"graceful-reload","text":"Graceful reload"},{"id":"blue-green","text":"Blue-green"},{"id":"canary-by-weight","text":"Canary by weight"},{"id":"drain-and-readiness","text":"Drain and readiness"},{"id":"incident-the-restart-that-dropped-uploads","text":"Incident: The restart that dropped uploads"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Controlling nginx","u":"https://nginx.org/en/docs/control.html"},{"t":"nginx split_clients","u":"https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_split_clients_module.html"}],"text":"The front door should never be closed for maintenance. Reloads, weights and drains let you change both the proxy and the services behind it while users keep working. What you will learn What a graceful reload does Blue-green switching and rollback Canary routing by weight, header or cookie Draining and readiness during deploys Graceful reload nginx -s reload (or systemctl reload nginx ) parses the new config. If it is valid, new workers start with it and old workers stop accepting new connections and exit when their current requests finish. If the new config is invalid, the old workers keep running. A restart would drop everything, so use reload for changes. Blue-green Run two complete environments, blue (live) and green (new). Test green privately, then switch the proxy&#39;s upstream in one reload. Rollback is switching back. upstream app_live { server 10.0.1.11:8080 ; server 10.0.1.12:8080 ; } # blue upstream app_next { server 10.0.2.11:8080 ; server 10.0.2.12:8080 ; } # green map $http_x_canary $pool { default app_live; \"1\" app_next; } server { location / { proxy_pass http:// $pool ; } } The header route lets testers reach green through the real proxy before anyone else does. Canary by weight split_clients \" ${remote_addr} ${http_user_agent} \" $pool { 5% app_next; * app_live; } Send 5 percent to the new version, watch error rate and latency, then 25, 50 and 100. Roll back by setting the share to zero. Shifting traffic safely Flow in order: 1, Proxy to Blue (live); 2, Proxy to Green (new); 3, Proxy to Error rate and latency; 4, Error rate and latency to Rollback. Step Network edge Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Blue (live) Step What it does: The current version serving everyone. If it fails or is weak: It must stay healthy until the new version is proven. Green (new) Step What it does: The new version, tested privately first. If it fails or is weak: An untested green receives real users at the switch. Proxy Network edge What it does: A reload switches or weights the upstream. If it fails or is weak: Restarting instead of reloading drops connections. Error rate and latency Observability What it does: Compare the new version with the old at each step. If it fails or is weak: No comparison means no signal to roll back. Rollback Step What it does: Return the share to blue in one reload. If it fails or is weak: A rollback that was never tested may fail when needed. Drain and readiness Remove a server from rotation, wait for current requests to finish, then stop it. A new server should join only after its readiness check passes. In Kubernetes this is what readiness probes and preStop hooks do for you; on plain hosts you do it with the proxy&#39;s drain or weight controls. Incident: The restart that dropped uploads Incident A config change applied with a service restart cut every open connection during business hours. $ systemctl restart nginx access.log: 1042 requests ended with status 499 client: net::ERR_CONNECTION_RESET Users lost uploads in progress. The team switched to nginx -t and reload for every change, and moved risky changes to a canary share first. Restart is reserved for upgrades, and happens only after drain. Common mistakes Restarting instead of reloading Switching all traffic at once with no way back Stopping a server before its requests finish Sending traffic to a server before it is ready"},"proxies/debugging-502-504-and-proxy-problems":{"html":"\u003cp>Proxy errors feel mysterious because the user, the proxy and the backend each see a different failure. A short method separates them: look at what the proxy logged, repeat the request directly to the backend, and compare.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What 502, 503, 504 and 499 each point to\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Logging upstream status and timing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Repeating the request around the proxy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Common causes: DNS, ports, timeouts, buffers, limits\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"read-the-status-code\">Read the status code\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Code\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>First place to look\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>400 / 413 / 431\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Bad request, body or headers too large\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Proxy limits, \u003ccode>client_max_body_size\u003c/code>, header buffers\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>499\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Client closed the connection\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Client timeouts, slow responses\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>502\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Bad gateway: no valid reply from upstream\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Is the backend up, right port, crash, wrong protocol\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>503\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>No upstream available, or overload\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Health checks, rate limits, backend capacity\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>504\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Gateway timeout\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Slow backend, timeout settings\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"log-what-matters\">Log what matters\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"nginx\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"log_format main '$remote_addr &quot;$request&quot; $status '\n                'rt=$request_time urt=$upstream_response_time us=$upstream_status ua=$upstream_addr';\naccess_log /var/log/nginx/access.log main;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">log_format\u003c/span> main \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$remote_addr\u003c/span> &quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$request\u003c/span>&quot; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$status\u003c/span> &#x27;\u003c/span>\n                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;rt=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$request_time\u003c/span> urt=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$upstream_response_time\u003c/span> us=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$upstream_status\u003c/span> ua=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$upstream_addr\u003c/span>&#x27;\u003c/span>;\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">access_log\u003c/span> /var/log/nginx/access.log main;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>urt\u003c/code> is the backend&#39;s time, \u003ccode>rt\u003c/code> the total. If \u003ccode>rt\u003c/code> is high but \u003ccode>urt\u003c/code> is low the delay is the client or the proxy; if both are high the backend is slow. \u003ccode>ua\u003c/code> names which server answered.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"go-around-the-proxy\">Go around the proxy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code} %{time_total}\\n' http://10.0.1.11:8080/healthz\ncurl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code} %{time_total}\\n' https://shop.example.com/api/orders\ntail -2 /var/log/nginx/error.log\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s -o /dev/null -w \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;%{http_code} %{time_total}\\n&#x27;\u003c/span> http://10.0.1.11:8080/healthz\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">200 0.004\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s -o /dev/null -w \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;%{http_code} %{time_total}\\n&#x27;\u003c/span> https://shop.example.com/api/orders\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">502 0.002\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -2 /var/log/nginx/error.log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">connect() failed (111: Connection refused) while connecting to upstream, upstream: &quot;http://10.0.1.12:8080/api/orders&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>If the direct request works and the proxied one fails, compare ports, hostnames, protocol (http versus https), headers and the upstream address in the error log. A fast 502 usually means connection refused; a slow 504 means the upstream was reached but did not answer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"frequent-causes\">Frequent causes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Stale DNS:\u003c/strong> nginx resolves upstream hostnames at startup. Use a resolver and a variable in proxy_pass, or reload after changes.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Wrong scheme or port:\u003c/strong> speaking HTTP to an HTTPS backend gives 502 with an SSL error in the log.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Backend crash or restart loop:\u003c/strong> check its logs and restart count.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Header too large:\u003c/strong> big cookies cause 400 or 502; raise \u003ccode>large_client_header_buffers\u003c/code> or shrink the cookie.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Limits:\u003c/strong> file descriptors, worker connections or backend pool exhausted.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"user&gt;plog;plog&gt;direct;direct&gt;cause;cause&gt;fix\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Narrowing a proxy error\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"87.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"user\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Status seen by the user\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"plog\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Proxy logs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"direct\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Request the backend directly\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cause\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cause\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"fix\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Fix and verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Status seen by the user to Proxy logs; 2, Proxy logs to Request the backend directly; 3, Request the backend directly to Cause; 4, Cause to Fix and verify.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"user\">\u003cdt>Status seen by the user \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> 502, 503, 504, 499 or 4xx tells which side to suspect.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> The same page can be a different cause each time.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"plog\">\u003cdt>Proxy logs \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> error.log reason and access.log upstream status and timing.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing upstream fields leave you guessing.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"direct\">\u003cdt>Request the backend directly \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Bypass the proxy to test the backend alone.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A direct test from another network may behave differently.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cause\">\u003cdt>Cause \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> DNS, port, protocol, timeout, header size or capacity.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Changing several settings hides which one mattered.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"fix\">\u003cdt>Fix and verify \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Change one thing and repeat the same request.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Without a repeat test the fix is only a guess.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-502-that-appeared-after-a-failover\">Incident: The 502 that appeared after a failover\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A database failover moved a backend to a new IP, and the proxy kept sending to the old address.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"dig +short app.internal\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">error.log: connect() failed (113: No route to host) while connecting to upstream, upstream: &quot;http://10.0.1.12:8080/&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>dig +short app.internal\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">10.0.1.30\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>nginx had resolved the name once at startup. A reload fixed it at once, and the long-term fix was to use a resolver with a short valid time and a variable in proxy_pass so the name is looked up again.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Debugging from the browser instead of the proxy log\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not logging upstream status and timing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Changing timeouts before knowing which side is slow\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that nginx caches upstream DNS names\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"read-the-status-code","text":"Read the status code"},{"id":"log-what-matters","text":"Log what matters"},{"id":"go-around-the-proxy","text":"Go around the proxy"},{"id":"frequent-causes","text":"Frequent causes"},{"id":"incident-the-502-that-appeared-after-a-failover","text":"Incident: The 502 that appeared after a failover"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"nginx error log","u":"https://nginx.org/en/docs/ngx_core_module.html#error_log"},{"t":"nginx log format variables","u":"https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_log_module.html"}],"text":"Proxy errors feel mysterious because the user, the proxy and the backend each see a different failure. A short method separates them: look at what the proxy logged, repeat the request directly to the backend, and compare. What you will learn What 502, 503, 504 and 499 each point to Logging upstream status and timing Repeating the request around the proxy Common causes: DNS, ports, timeouts, buffers, limits Read the status code Code Meaning First place to look 400 / 413 / 431 Bad request, body or headers too large Proxy limits, client_max_body_size , header buffers 499 Client closed the connection Client timeouts, slow responses 502 Bad gateway: no valid reply from upstream Is the backend up, right port, crash, wrong protocol 503 No upstream available, or overload Health checks, rate limits, backend capacity 504 Gateway timeout Slow backend, timeout settings Log what matters log_format main &#x27; $remote_addr \" $request \" $status &#x27; &#x27;rt= $request_time urt= $upstream_response_time us= $upstream_status ua= $upstream_addr &#x27; ; access_log /var/log/nginx/access.log main; urt is the backend&#39;s time, rt the total. If rt is high but urt is low the delay is the client or the proxy; if both are high the backend is slow. ua names which server answered. Go around the proxy $ curl -s -o /dev/null -w &#x27;%{http_code} %{time_total}\\n&#x27; http://10.0.1.11:8080/healthz 200 0.004 $ curl -s -o /dev/null -w &#x27;%{http_code} %{time_total}\\n&#x27; https://shop.example.com/api/orders 502 0.002 $ tail -2 /var/log/nginx/error.log connect() failed (111: Connection refused) while connecting to upstream, upstream: \"http://10.0.1.12:8080/api/orders\" If the direct request works and the proxied one fails, compare ports, hostnames, protocol (http versus https), headers and the upstream address in the error log. A fast 502 usually means connection refused; a slow 504 means the upstream was reached but did not answer. Frequent causes Stale DNS: nginx resolves upstream hostnames at startup. Use a resolver and a variable in proxy_pass, or reload after changes. Wrong scheme or port: speaking HTTP to an HTTPS backend gives 502 with an SSL error in the log. Backend crash or restart loop: check its logs and restart count. Header too large: big cookies cause 400 or 502; raise large_client_header_buffers or shrink the cookie. Limits: file descriptors, worker connections or backend pool exhausted. Narrowing a proxy error Flow in order: 1, Status seen by the user to Proxy logs; 2, Proxy logs to Request the backend directly; 3, Request the backend directly to Cause; 4, Cause to Fix and verify. Client Observability Service Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Status seen by the user Client What it does: 502, 503, 504, 499 or 4xx tells which side to suspect. If it fails or is weak: The same page can be a different cause each time. Proxy logs Observability What it does: error.log reason and access.log upstream status and timing. If it fails or is weak: Missing upstream fields leave you guessing. Request the backend directly Service What it does: Bypass the proxy to test the backend alone. If it fails or is weak: A direct test from another network may behave differently. Cause Step What it does: DNS, port, protocol, timeout, header size or capacity. If it fails or is weak: Changing several settings hides which one mattered. Fix and verify Step What it does: Change one thing and repeat the same request. If it fails or is weak: Without a repeat test the fix is only a guess. Incident: The 502 that appeared after a failover Incident A database failover moved a backend to a new IP, and the proxy kept sending to the old address. error.log: connect() failed (113: No route to host) while connecting to upstream, upstream: \"http://10.0.1.12:8080/\" $ dig +short app.internal 10.0.1.30 nginx had resolved the name once at startup. A reload fixed it at once, and the long-term fix was to "}});